<p>The easiest way to clean an oven at home is to coat the inside with a paste of bicarbonate of soda and water, leave it overnight, then wipe it out and finish with a light spray of diluted white vinegar. Soak the racks separately and give the door glass its own attention, and most ovens come up far better than you'd expect.</p>
<p>That approach suits an oven that gets cleaned now and then. If yours has years of baked-on carbon, DIY turns into a long, messy job, and that's usually the point where people book <a href="{{service:oven-cleaning}}">professional oven cleaning</a> instead.</p>
<p>Our cleaners work on all sorts of ovens across Hull and East Yorkshire, from compact built-in singles in city-centre flats to big range cookers in farmhouse kitchens. Here's what genuinely works at home, and how to tell when it's time to hand the job over.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Bicarbonate of soda paste left overnight shifts most everyday grease without harsh fumes.</li><li>Soak racks and trays separately; they're usually the slowest part of the job.</li><li>Never scrub catalytic liners or let caustic cleaner touch seals, elements or the fan.</li><li>Heavily carbonised ovens are quicker and safer to hand to a professional.</li></ul></div>
<h2>What you need to clean an oven at home</h2>
<p>You don't need a cupboard full of specialist products. For the bicarb method, gather:</p>
<ul>
<li>Bicarbonate of soda (a full tub for a heavily used oven)</li>
<li>White vinegar in a spray bottle, diluted roughly half and half with water</li>
<li>Rubber gloves, plus an old towel or newspaper for the floor</li>
<li>A plastic scraper or old bank card for loosening carbon</li>
<li>Non-scratch scourer pads and a few microfibre cloths</li>
<li>A bowl of warm water with a squeeze of washing-up liquid</li>
</ul>
<p>Before you start, make sure the oven is switched off and completely cold. Check the manual too. Some ovens have catalytic liners or a self-clean setting that changes what you should and shouldn't do, which we cover further down. If you prefer gentler products around the house, our guide to <a href="{{guide:gentle-cleaning-hard-water-homes}}">eco-friendly cleaning that actually works</a> explains why bicarb and vinegar earn their place.</p>
<h2>How to clean an oven with bicarbonate of soda, step by step</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Empty it.</strong> Take out the racks, shelves and trays and set them aside for soaking.</li>
<li><strong>Remove loose debris.</strong> Brush or hoover out crumbs and flakes of burnt food so you're not just turning them into paste.</li>
<li><strong>Make the paste.</strong> Mix bicarbonate of soda with a little water until it's spreadable, roughly the texture of toothpaste.</li>
<li><strong>Coat the inside.</strong> Spread it over the base, sides, back and inside of the door, avoiding the heating elements, the fan and the rubber door seal. Go thicker on the worst patches.</li>
<li><strong>Leave it overnight.</strong> Around twelve hours gives the bicarb time to soften the grease. The paste will turn brown as it works.</li>
<li><strong>Scrape and wipe.</strong> Use the plastic scraper on stubborn areas, then wipe the paste away with a damp cloth, rinsing the cloth often.</li>
<li><strong>Spray with vinegar.</strong> A light mist of diluted vinegar fizzes against leftover bicarb and helps lift the white residue.</li>
<li><strong>Rinse and dry.</strong> Wipe everything with clean water, dry with a microfibre cloth, then run the oven briefly on a low heat to drive off moisture before you cook.</li>
</ol>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Hard water can leave a chalky film after rinsing. A final wipe with diluted vinegar and a dry cloth stops the inside of the oven looking streaky.</p></div>
<h2>How to clean oven racks and trays</h2>
<p>Racks are usually the slowest part of the job, because grease bakes into every joint. Soaking does most of the work for you.</p>
<ol>
<li>Lay an old towel in the bottom of the bath to protect it from scratches.</li>
<li>Add enough hot water to cover the racks and dissolve a few dishwasher tablets or a scoop of biological washing powder in it.</li>
<li>Leave them overnight, then scrub with a non-scratch scourer or a stiff brush.</li>
<li>Rinse thoroughly, dry and refit.</li>
</ol>
<p>Baking trays and grill pans can go in the same soak. Avoid wire wool on non-stick or enamel trays, as it scratches the surface and makes food stick more next time.</p>
<h2>How to clean oven door glass</h2>
<p>The inside of the door glass collects a brown, sticky film that bicarb paste handles well. Apply it, leave it for at least twenty minutes, then work it off with a damp cloth. A glass-safe scraper blade, held flat against the glass, removes stubborn spots without scratching.</p>
<h3>Cleaning between the glass panes</h3>
<p>Streaks between the panes come from grease and steam getting through small gaps. On many ovens the door lifts off and the inner glass slides or unscrews out, but the method varies by make, so check your manual first. If you're not confident, leave it: a cracked inner pane is a costly part to replace.</p>
<h2>Oven cleaner sprays, self-cleaning ovens and catalytic liners</h2>
<h3>Caustic oven cleaners</h3>
<p>Strong supermarket oven cleaners work quickly, but most are caustic. They need gloves, eye protection and good ventilation. Keep them off the door seal, heating elements, fan, any aluminium parts and the outside of the oven, and never mix them with other products. Read the label in full and keep children and pets out of the kitchen while you use them.</p>
<h3>Pyrolytic (self-cleaning) ovens</h3>
<p>Pyrolytic ovens heat to a very high temperature and turn food residue to ash, which you then wipe out. Remove the racks and anything else the manual tells you to, open a window, and expect a smell during the cycle. Don't use oven cleaner on pyrolytic enamel unless the manufacturer says it's safe.</p>
<h3>Catalytic liners</h3>
<p>Catalytic liners are the rough-textured panels on the sides or back of some ovens. They absorb and break down grease when the oven runs hot. Never scrub them or spray them with oven cleaner, which ruins the coating. If they've turned dark and shiny, they may need replacing rather than cleaning.</p>
<h2>Common oven cleaning mistakes to avoid</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cleaning a warm oven.</strong> Wait until it's fully cold. Products dry out too fast and you risk burns.</li>
<li><strong>Using wire wool on enamel or glass.</strong> It leaves fine scratches that trap grease and make the next clean harder.</li>
<li><strong>Soaking the door seal in chemicals.</strong> A damaged seal lets heat escape and can affect how evenly the oven cooks.</li>
<li><strong>Leaving residue behind.</strong> Any cleaner left inside will smoke and smell the next time you cook, so rinse thoroughly.</li>
<li><strong>Forgetting the hob and extractor.</strong> A spotless oven under a greasy extractor filter still makes the kitchen look tired. Our <a href="{{guide:kitchen-deep-clean-checklist}}">kitchen deep clean checklist</a> covers the rest of the room.</li>
</ul>
<p>Little and often makes every clean easier. Our guide on <a href="{{guide:signs-oven-needs-professional-clean}}">how often you should clean your oven</a> explains the warning signs and a sensible routine.</p>
<h2>DIY vs professional oven cleaning: which is right for you?</h2>
<p>DIY makes sense when the oven is in reasonable shape and you have an evening and a morning to spare. A professional clean is worth considering when:</p>
<ul>
<li>There's thick black carbon that bicarb won't shift</li>
<li>You'd rather not use caustic products and their fumes at home</li>
<li>The door glass needs taking apart</li>
<li>You're moving out and the oven has to pass an inventory check. Ovens are one of the first things letting agents look at, which is why they feature heavily in our <a href="{{guide:end-of-tenancy-cleaning-checklist}}">end of tenancy cleaning checklist</a> and are included in every <a href="{{service:end-of-tenancy-cleaning}}">end of tenancy clean</a> we carry out</li>
<li>You have a range cooker or AGA with several cavities, which our <a href="{{guide:aga-range-cooker-cleaning}}">AGA and range cooker cleaning guide</a> covers separately</li>
</ul>
<p>Our oven cleaning uses fume-free, non-caustic products. Racks, trays and removable parts are cleaned separately, the door glass is cleaned inside and out, and you can usually cook again the same day. If you're planning your day around it, see <a href="{{guide:how-to-clean-an-oven}}">how long professional oven cleaning takes</a>.</p>
<h2>Getting your oven cleaned properly</h2>
<p>If the bicarb method hasn't done the job, or you'd rather not spend a weekend on your knees in front of the oven, our team can take it off your hands. We cover <a href="{{area:hull}}">Hull</a>, <a href="{{area:beverley}}">Beverley</a> and the surrounding towns, bring all our own equipment and agree a fixed quote before we start.</p>
<p>You can book an <a href="{{service:oven-cleaning}}">oven clean</a> on its own or pair it with a <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep clean</a> of the whole kitchen. <a href="{{page:contact}}">Get in touch</a> with the make and model of your oven and we'll take it from there.</p>
