		'content'     => <<<'HTML'
<p>To clean an AGA, wipe the enamel with a damp cloth as you go, brush carbonised spills off the hotplates with a wire brush, and let the hot ovens burn off most food residue before brushing it out. A range cooker is cleaned more like a standard oven, just with more cavities, more racks and sometimes catalytic liners that must never be scrubbed.</p>
<p>Both are hard-wearing cookers that can still be damaged by the wrong products. They're a common sight in farmhouse kitchens, market-town homes and period properties around Hull and East Yorkshire. This guide covers how to keep yours looking good, and when professional <a href="{{service:oven-cleaning}}">AGA and range cooker cleaning</a> makes more sense than doing it yourself.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Traditional AGA ovens burn off most spills themselves; brush the carbon out rather than scrubbing.</li><li>Never use abrasive pads or caustic oven cleaner on vitreous enamel or catalytic liners.</li><li>Wipe acidic or sugary spills off enamel promptly, before they mark it.</li><li>A traditional AGA needs switching off and cooling before a full deep clean, which takes many hours.</li></ul></div>
<h2>How AGAs differ from ordinary ovens</h2>
<p>A traditional AGA is a heat-storage cooker. Its heavy cast-iron ovens stay hot all the time, with a very hot roasting oven, a cooler simmering oven and, on larger models, baking and warming ovens. The hotplates on top sit under insulated lids that keep the heat in when you're not cooking.</p>
<p>That constant heat is good news for cleaning. Spills in the roasting oven gradually carbonise into a dry ash that can simply be brushed out. The cooler ovens don't burn residue off as effectively, so they need more attention. Newer electric and programmable AGA models heat up and cool down much faster, which makes them easier to switch off for a deep clean.</p>
<h2>How to clean an AGA: daily and weekly care</h2>
<h3>The enamel top and front</h3>
<p>The glossy vitreous enamel is tough but not indestructible. Wipe it with a soft, damp microfibre cloth and a little washing-up liquid, then buff dry. For marks that won't shift, use a mild cream cleaner that's suitable for enamel. Deal with spills of milk, fruit juice, jam, vinegar and fat quickly, as acidic and sugary spills can dull or mark the finish if they're left to bake on.</p>
<h3>Hotplates and lids</h3>
<p>Cast-iron hotplates are cleaned with the stiff wire brush supplied with most AGAs. Brush off carbonised food when it builds up, rather than using water or chemicals on the hot surface. Wipe the tops of the lids with a damp cloth and polish with a product suitable for chrome or stainless steel. The inside lining of each lid can be wiped gently once the lid has cooled.</p>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> A kettle that lives on the AGA hotplate builds up limescale quickly in hard water areas. Our <a href="{{guide:hard-water-limescale-east-yorkshire}}">guide to limescale and hard water in East Yorkshire</a> explains how to descale it safely.</p></div>
<h3>The ovens</h3>
<p>Every so often, brush out the carbon from the roasting oven with the wire brush and a dustpan. On many models the oven doors lift off their hinges so you can clean the inner door lining, but they're heavy and very hot on a running AGA. Use thick oven gloves, lay the door face down on a towel and check your manual first.</p>
<h2>Deep cleaning an AGA: switching off and cooling down</h2>
<p>Day-to-day wiping can be done while the cooker is running. A proper deep clean of the ovens, door linings, hotplates and the area around them is safer and more thorough once a traditional AGA has been switched off and allowed to cool fully. That can take many hours, so most owners turn it off the evening before.</p>
<p>Some older oil and gas models are fiddly to relight, so check your manual or ask your AGA engineer before you turn it off. Many owners time a deep clean around a routine service, when the cooker is off anyway. Cleaning is not a substitute for servicing, and any work on burners, flues or fuel supplies should be left to a qualified engineer.</p>
<h2>How to clean a range cooker</h2>
<p>Range cookers from makers such as Rangemaster, Falcon and Stoves work like conventional ovens, so the methods in our guide on <a href="{{guide:how-to-clean-an-oven}}">how to clean an oven</a> apply. There's just more of everything: two or three oven cavities, a separate grill compartment, a large hob and plenty of racks.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Work one cavity at a time.</strong> Remove the racks and shelf supports, apply bicarbonate of soda paste to the enamel and leave it to work.</li>
<li><strong>Check for catalytic liners.</strong> Many range cookers have rough-textured liners in the main oven. Don't scrub them or use oven cleaner on them.</li>
<li><strong>Soak the pan supports.</strong> Cast-iron pan supports can be soaked in hot soapy water, then dried thoroughly so they don't rust.</li>
<li><strong>Clean the burners.</strong> On gas models, lift off the burner caps and heads, clean them with a soft brush and warm soapy water, and make sure they're completely dry before refitting so they light evenly.</li>
<li><strong>Finish the outside.</strong> Clean the control knobs, handles, splashback and the front of the doors, using products suited to enamel, stainless steel or painted finishes.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Products and tools to avoid on AGAs and range cookers</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Caustic oven cleaner</strong> on AGA enamel, catalytic liners or painted finishes</li>
<li><strong>Wire wool and abrasive scourers</strong> on enamel, glass or stainless steel</li>
<li><strong>Bleach or limescale remover</strong> left sitting on enamel or chrome</li>
<li><strong>Soaking wet cloths</strong> pushed into controls or around gas burners</li>
<li><strong>Steam cleaners</strong> on a hot AGA or near electrical parts</li>
</ul>
<p>When in doubt, the manufacturer's care guide is the final word. Using the wrong product can leave permanent dull patches that no amount of cleaning will fix.</p>
<h2>What a professional AGA or range cooker clean includes</h2>
<p>A professional clean follows the same careful approach as a standard oven clean, using fume-free, non-caustic products that are safe on enamel. For a range cooker, that means each cavity, the grill compartment, racks, trays, door glass and the hob. For an AGA, it means brushing out and cleaning the ovens, door linings, hotplates and lids, and bringing the enamel back to a shine.</p>
<p>Because there's more to do, these jobs take longer than a standard single or double oven. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:how-to-clean-an-oven}}">how long oven cleaning takes</a> gives typical times, and we'll confirm an estimate once we know your model. To keep the cooker in good condition between visits, our advice on <a href="{{guide:signs-oven-needs-professional-clean}}">how often to clean your oven</a> applies to range cookers too.</p>
<h2>Range cookers in holiday lets and rental homes</h2>
<p>Holiday cottages in places like <a href="{{area:driffield}}">Driffield</a>, <a href="{{area:pocklington}}">Pocklington</a> and along the coast often have a range cooker as the centrepiece of the kitchen. Guests use them hard and rarely clean them the way an owner would. A thorough clean between seasons, plus a quick check at every changeover, keeps reviews and inventories on track. Our <a href="{{guide:holiday-let-changeover-checklist}}">holiday let changeover checklist</a> shows where the cooker fits into a turnaround, and we can build a deep clean into regular <a href="{{service:holiday-let-airbnb-cleaning}}">holiday let cleaning</a>.</p>
<h2>Booking an AGA or range cooker clean</h2>
<p>If your range cooker has got beyond a quick wipe, or you'd like the AGA properly cleaned before guests or a family gathering, our team can help. We bring our own equipment, agree a fixed quote before starting and can combine the cooker with a wider <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">kitchen deep clean</a>, following our <a href="{{guide:kitchen-deep-clean-checklist}}">kitchen deep clean checklist</a>.</p>
<p>Read more about our <a href="{{service:oven-cleaning}}">oven and range cooker cleaning</a>, or <a href="{{page:contact}}">get in touch</a> with the make and model of your cooker so we can plan the visit.</p>
